 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Health, medicine & food safety",
  "title": "Measles, medicines and malaria: a health day across three countries",
  "body": "**The US loses measles elimination.** On Nov 17 PAHO formally revoked the status the US had held since 2000, after a year with 5,412 cases, 10 deaths and 48 outbreaks (Confirmed). A joint CDC, HHS and PAHO statement lists $100 million in extra federal money since August, split roughly evenly between outbreak response and lab and epidemiology capacity, plus field teams sent to six states. Pennsylvania and New York are now asking for help, and New York is under a state of emergency (Reported, ABC). The statement cites a Johns Hopkins study finding falling MMR coverage in 78% of 2,066 counties. Five public-health associations want a funded national plan that aims for 95% coverage.\n\n**Poland: drug prices and alcohol adverts.** The reimbursement bill, with about 175 changes, went back to the Sejm Health Committee after its second reading. It includes a Critical Medicines List and a bigger price discount for drugs made in Poland. Today the same committee takes up the Senate's amendments to the anti-alcohol law. One of them, passed in the Senate by a single vote, would allow adverts for 0% beer, which the Health Ministry opposes. No new GIS food recall was found in the window.\n\n**India: malaria gains under threat.** Opening the 50th-anniversary year of ICMR's malaria institute, President Murmu said climate change, unplanned urbanisation and insecticide resistance could undo India's progress (Confirmed, ThePrint).\n\n**Science & space.** NASA will end science operations on the 27-year-old Terra Earth-observation satellite on Dec 1. Poland and India were quiet on science.",
